{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1528874000,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUzMzE4NjkxOTQ=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2657","origin":"github"},"title":"Paradigm shift: --nosave -\u003e --amend ?!","message":"Over the past year we have gravitated towards a model where we recommend  (and sometimes require) datasets to be clean. In the context of #2428 I keep thinking about one source of complications that we are \"causing\" ourselves: `add --nosave`.\n\nThis is a very useful feature for people that care about \"readability\" of a datasets history. I use this pretty much every day. However, I noticed that I do the following:\n\n```\ndatalad add ... --nosave\n...\ngit commit\n```\n\nwhere the first command stages my addition, and the final commit creates the commit and gets the dataset clean. I do this, because it is the only reliable way to achieve the outcome, given the various complications in the behavior of `add` and `save.\n\nThis has two issues:\n\n1. It breaks the paradigm, as the dataset is not clean in between the command (which might correspond to a loooong time, with intermediate memory loss on what this was about).\n2. it pushes me (and by extension any datalad user) to use Git directly instead of DataLad\n\nI propose to:\n\n1. discontinue the `save=` option\n2. replace it (and potentially enhance other commands) with an `--amend` flag.\n\nThis will achieve a few things:\n- anyone can commit any change right away, and incrementally build up changesets with `--amend` across individual command calls\n- as a consequence a dataset will tend to be clean more often\n- users can stay in the DataLad world for this basic management task\n\nIf we go down this path, there is even less reason for keeping `add/save` as two separate commands.","files":null}]}